From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 15:15:58 +0200
Subject: media: gc2145: Keep the field of view at every resolution

The readout window was sized from the requested resolution, so anything
smaller than the full frame was a window onto the middle of the pixel
array. 1280x720 read out 1296x752, which is 81 percent of the width and
63 percent of the height: the picture was not a smaller version of the
full frame, it was a crop of it.

The sensor has three ways of making the output smaller that keep the
whole array in the picture, and only one of them was used. Read out the
analogue crop in full and pick a decimation instead:

  - row skip drops every other row in the array, paired with the ISP
    column scaler so the pixels stay square. It is the only one that
    shortens the frame, which is what makes 30fps possible at all.
  - the subsample block decimates by an arbitrary ratio after that.

The ratios on offer are the ones that divide the active area exactly,
so the ISP crop only has to trim the dummy pixels: 1600x1200, 1280x960,
800x600, 640x480, 400x300 and 320x240. The last four halve the frame
period, so at 96MHz they reach 30fps where the first two reach 15.

The analogue crop becomes a settable selection rectangle, so a caller
that does want a narrower field of view can ask for one and see what it
got, and enum_frame_size follows it.

The subsample position lists are not documented beyond a handful of
examples. The one ratio a vendor table exercises, 4/5 for its 720p
mode, comes out of this code byte for byte identical.

Signed-off-by: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
